    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the list. one more thing—is there a way to tell if a packaged food is going to be high or low GI just by reading the label? like, are there specific numbers or ingredients i should watch out for that aren't super obvious?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Unfortunately, the GI score isn't always listed directly on a food label, so it's not always an obvious metric. However, you should watch for ingredients like refined white flour, added sugars (like high-fructose corn syrup), and white rice, as these often point to higher GI content. Conversely, whole grain certification and the presence of high amounts of soluble fiber are strong indicators of a lower GI food. Ultimately, focusing on the *type* of processing is often a better guide than looking for a single number.",
